vimrc: set hidden

@brief
Your MR came back with four comments. None of them is an argument;
all four are "yes, fine, change it". The job is to turn them around
before standup.

    !312  Order report provider

    @rk   Line 9 and the two filters: `==` should be `===`. We have
          the lint rule off in this directory, which is its own ticket.

    @rk   Three console.logs left in. They go.

    @sd   `findByCustomer` takes the id directly since !298. Both call
          sites here still pass an object. Update them.

    @rk   The keys of the returned object in `dailyTotals` should be in
          alphabetical order so the snapshot test stops churning.

`order.repository.ts` is open so you can check the new signature. It is
not yours to change.

Four comments, four different tools. Reaching for the same hammer on all
four is what makes a review turnaround take an hour.

@keys
| `:%s/old/new/g` | every occurrence in the file |
| `:g/pat/d` | delete every line matching a pattern |
| `ci(` then `n` `.` | change inside the parens, then again at the next match |
| `:.,+2sort` | sort this line and the two below it |

@hints
Take the comments one at a time and ask what shape each change is: many
identical edits, whole lines to remove, a handful of edits at matches you
can search for, or a block that needs reordering. Each shape has a tool.
--
Identical text in many places is `:%s`. Whole lines identified by a
pattern is `:g//d`. Reordering lines is `:sort` over a range. The two
call sites are close enough to each other that search plus `.` beats
writing a substitution you have to get exactly right.
--
`:%s/ == / === /g`, then `:g/console\.log/d`. For the call sites, search
for `customerId: args`, which lands you inside the object literal, and
`ci(` replaces the whole argument list. `n` then `.` does the second one.
For the keys, land on the `shipped:` line and `:.,+2sort`.

@solution
99 keystrokes, in the order the comments arrived.

**`==` to `===`** — `:%s/ == / === /g`. The spaces are the guard. A bare
`:%s/==/===/g` is a well-known way to turn `===` into `=====` on a second
run, and here it would also be tempted by nothing else in the file, which
is exactly the kind of luck you should not depend on.

**the logging** — `:g/console\.log/d`. Three lines, three different
places, one pattern. This is the shape `:g` exists for: the lines have
nothing in common except the thing you are searching for.

**the call sites** — `/customerId: args` `Enter` puts the cursor inside
the object literal, `ci(args.customerId` `Esc` replaces the entire
argument list, brackets and all. Then `n` `.`. Two edits, and the second
one costs two keystrokes.

You could have done this with a substitution too. Try writing it: the
pattern has to cover `({ customerId: args.customerId })` including the
braces and the spaces, and you will spend longer getting it right than
the edit takes. When there are two of something, search and dot wins.
Somewhere around five it flips.

**the keys** — `/shipped:` `Enter` `:.,+2sort`. Sorting three lines by
hand means three cut-and-pastes and a reread to check you got it right.
`:sort` over a range cannot get it wrong.

The meta-lesson: a review turnaround is four small jobs of four different
shapes, not one big edit. Naming the shape before you start — many,
lines, a few, order — picks the tool for you, and picking the tool is
most of the speed.

@start src/domain/report/order.report.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderReportProvider {
  constructor(@inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async dailyTotals(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: args.customerId });
    console.log('orders', orders.length);

    const shipped = orders.filter((order) => order.status == 'SHIPPED');
    const pending = orders.filter((order) => order.status == 'PENDING');
    const cancelled = orders.filter((order) => order.status == 'CANCELLED');
    console.log('cancelled', cancelled.length);

    return {
      shipped: shipped.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0),
      pending: pending.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0),
      cancelled: cancelled.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0),
    };
  }

  public async refundRate(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: args.customerId });
    console.log('refundRate', orders.length);

    const refunded = orders.filter((order) => order.status == 'CANCELLED');

    return refunded.length / orders.length;
  }
}

@expected src/domain/report/order.report.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderReportProvider {
  constructor(@inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async dailyTotals(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer(args.customerId);

    const shipped = orders.filter((order) => order.status === 'SHIPPED');
    const pending = orders.filter((order) => order.status === 'PENDING');
    const cancelled = orders.filter((order) => order.status === 'CANCELLED');

    return {
      cancelled: cancelled.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0),
      pending: pending.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0),
      shipped: shipped.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0),
    };
  }

  public async refundRate(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer(args.customerId);

    const refunded = orders.filter((order) => order.status === 'CANCELLED');

    return refunded.length / orders.length;
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}
